I"#<p>On Your Mark72—I Purposely Distorted the Lyrics for This Film</p>

<p>Animage, September 1995</p>

<p>—“Police Officer,” “Angel”—they make it sound like a film by Mamoru Oshii-san.</p>

<p>Miyazaki: Oshii-san is playing coy whether an angel is born or not, so I just went ahead and got this out. [laughs] I’m not saying it’s an angel, it might be a bird-person. It doesn’t matter either way.</p>

<p>—It felt like an entire feature film’s worth of content was packed into six minutes and forty seconds.</p>

<p>Miyazaki: I’ve put in a lot of coded meanings, but it’s a music video so people can take it in any way they want and I don’t care.</p>

<p>—What is that strange building in the quiet countryside at the beginning?</p>

<p>Miyazaki: You can interpret it anyway you want. I just hope people will have some general recognition when they see the truck with the nuclear hazard sign that comes up just after that scene. Radiation has permeated the ground, making it impossible for humans to live there. But it’s surrounded by greenery, like the area around Chernobyl. It has transformed into a nature sanctuary. The human beings have built a city underground where they live.</p>

<p>In reality, I don’t think we could live that way, so we’d probably be living above ground and getting sick.</p>

<p>—This animation was made as a music video for “On Your Mark,” wasn’t it?</p>

<p>Miyazaki: The title is from the phrase “on your mark, get set, go” but I’ve purposely distorted the meaning of the phrase in making this film. I made it a story set after the end of the century. It’s set in a world where radiation proliferates and sickness spreads. I think this time will actually come. I made this film when I was wondering what it would mean to live in a place like that.</p>

<p>Anarchy would be widespread in such an age, and the authorities would be very conservative about any criticism of the establishment. This is because they would think they still had something to lose: if all is lost, anarchy reigns and people start dying in the streets. What distracts people from these conditions are drugs, professional sports, or religion; doesn’t this sound true? They all proliferate. In the kind of age that I’m describing, it occurred to me that people might try to hide what they want to say from the authorities by expressing it in a song with coded words. So this is a somewhat subversive film. [laughs]</p>

<p>—For example, in the phrase, “whenever I ran I caught the cold going around,” does the “cold going around” mean the world covered by radiation and sickness?</p>

<p>Miyazaki [neither agreeing nor denying]: After all, for the entire history of earth, the problems of people have been like a cold going around.</p>

<p>—The angel saved by the two policemen can be seen as a thread of hope in this chaotic world. As in the phrase, “even so we won’t stop …” the scene of rescuing the angel is repeated many times; after many failures, she flies into the blue sky like a shaft of hope rising up from a chaotic world. But the policemen are left behind on the ground …</p>

<p>Miyazaki: I’m not suggesting that she was a savior or that there was any sort of heartfelt exchange with her after her rescue. What I am saying, though, is that if I had my own dream or hope—something that I refused to allow anyone else to touch—and without totally surrendering to the situation I let go of it, I would want to let it go somewhere no one else could reach it. That is what this is. There might have been a minute heartfelt exchange at the instant of release. That is all, that is enough … The policemen will try to go back to working as policemen. Of course, I don’t know if they’ll really be able to. [laughs]</p>

<p>—The world to which they’ll return is the world of “the cold going around.”</p>

<p>Miyazaki: In the end, that’s where we must start from each time. Even in a chaotic, ruined world, there will always be good things and exciting things. As in Nausicaä, “We are like birds, forever going beyond that morning, spitting out blood as we fly.”</p>

